Aha! `.power-out` base class has `opacity: 0` but computed opacity is 1 despite no `show` class! Why? The CSS `.power-out` sets opacity 0, transition 1.8s. Computed opacity 1 means something overrides. Maybe a later rule sets `.power-out { opacity: 1 }`? Search styles for "power-out". Only lines 476-488. But maybe `.scare` body class or something? Wait computed opacity 1 with transition means transition from 0 to 1? There's no class change. Or CSS specificity from `.power-out-copy`? No.

Look at `.power-out.show` not present. Could an animation be applied? bodyClass empty. Hmm.

Maybe the media query end rules? no.

Wait, the screenshot shows power offline overlay at maybe 50% opacity, not fully (text visible, background darkening). The computed opacity 1 could be inherited? opacity doesn't inherit.

Could the CSS `opacity: 0` line be inside a block that isn't applied due to malformed CSS? Let's inspect exact CSS around 476 and check if a syntax error preceding it swallowed declarations. The `.power-out` rule at 476:
```
.power-out {
  position: fixed; inset: 0; z-index: 17; pointer-events: none;
  background: rgba(0,0,0,.66);
  display: grid; place-items: center; opacity: 0; transition: opacity 1.8s ease;
}
```
That's correct. Computed opacity 1 means maybe transition animation from 0 -> 1 is triggered because class `show` was added and removed quickly? No. Even if removed, transition would go back to 0 over 1.8s; page loaded 2s. Not.

Maybe there is JavaScript setting style.opacity? Search `powerOut.style`.

