content.text:
Visibility computed visible, because `.power-out.show`? class has no show. Why visibility visible? Maybe initial transition includes visibility? Our transition on base `visibility 0s linear 1.8s` from initial visible to hidden; at time 0 of transition still visible. Since transition stuck at 0ms (headless slow), visibility remains visible. Ugh. The FOUC transition from initial styles persists because initial visibility transition also triggered. To avoid any transition on page load entirely, use `display:none` by default and a `.show` class with `display:grid`, but that kills fade. Alternatively set base `display:none`, and `.show { display:grid }`, then use `@starting-style`? Not needed. For power outage, fade in is nice but not critical. Could use `opacity:0; visibility:hidden;` plus `.show` and a `body.loaded`? Hmm.

Better approach: add a class `power-out` with opacity 0 and visibility hidden, but avoid transition being applied initially by using an animation for show instead of transition:
```
.power-out { opacity:0; visibility:hidden; ... }
.power-out.show { visibility:visible; animation: powerFade 1.8s ease forwards; }
@keyframes powerFade { from {opacity:0} to {opacity:1} }
```
Still on page load, base visibility hidden immediately (no transition property), so invisible. But does base opacity 0 get applied? There's no transition so computed should 0, visibility hidden. Great. On show, animation from 0 to 1, visibility visible. On remove, visibility hidden immediately (could be abrupt but okay, power out only resets when blackout/jumpscare). We can add fade-out animation? Removal occurs when starting a new night or menu; abrupt not noticeable behind transition to menu/black screen.

Use animation. Note multiple nights: `.show` animation runs each time class is added (yes). Keep `transition` removed. Let's replace.

